Simple linear laceration from kitchen knife slip to non-dominant index finger
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Smooth straight digital laceration, Intact two-point discrimination, Culinary slip mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-022
Reason for Visit: Acute digital laceration requiring urgent assessment for nerve or tendon damage and primary closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, bleeding knife wound to the index finger. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to perform digital neurovascular clearance, clear underlying structural tendon integrity, and execute definitive hemostatic wound closure prior to daytime primary care availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I slipped while chopping vegetables and sliced my index finger open; it stings sharply and won't stop bleeding.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a history of chopping food and slipping with a sharp culinary blade, resulting in an immediate, sharply demarcated straight laceration on the lateral aspect of the non-dominant index finger. This was accompanied by immediate sharp pain coupled with brisk localized capillary bleeding. The patient notes the bleeding has slowed but continuously oozes upon movement. Patient denies any loss of sensation or difficulty bending the fingertip.
Functional Status: Holding the finger in mild flexion, avoiding active use.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor interruption to meal preparation; no ongoing environmental hazards.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/78 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, localized discomfort isolated to the affected digit.
Physical Exam: Digital examination reveals a cleanly demarcated straight laceration on the lateral aspect of the digit. The wound edges are smooth, linear, and easily opposable with gentle tension. Direct clinical visualization establishes superficial tissue involvement. There is preservation of full, pain-free active flexion at both the proximal and distal interphalangeal joints. Normal two-point sensory discrimination is noted on both the radial and ulnar digital nerve distributions. Distal digital capillary refill is intact (<2 seconds) with normal pulp turgor. Complete absence of a visible severed tendon end retracting within the wound bed. Complete absence of bony crepitus or step-off palpable beneath the dermal laceration.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen counter wiped clean by patient.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple linear laceration to non-dominant index finger - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Digital nerve laceration - Ruled out due to: Normal, symmetrical two-point discrimination on the volar pad.

Flexor digitorum profundus laceration - Ruled out due to: Intact and strong isolated active flexion of the distal interphalangeal joint.

Underlying phalangeal fracture - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of palpable bony step-off, normal alignment, and simple sharp blade mechanism lacking blunt force.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A culinary knife slip to the digit requires rigorous exclusion of underlying tendon and digital nerve lacerations. Bedside evaluation confidently cleared these structures, confirming an isolated simple dermal laceration. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to execute necessary high-pressure decontamination of food particulates, achieve definitive primary closure, and restore functional confidence, effectively treating the injury safely in the home and preventing delayed wound infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wound irrigated with normal saline. Primary closure achieved with tissue adhesive (cyanoacrylate) and reinforced with steri-strips, as the wound edges were easily approximated without high tension. Tetanus status up to date.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immobilize the injured finger alongside the adjacent finger using medical tape (buddy-taping) for 3 days. This severely restricts isolated bending of the lacerated skin, directly preventing the wound glue from cracking and the cut from reopening.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Finger splint/buddy tape.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days for a wound check.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local GP if the finger becomes increasingly hot, red, significantly swollen, or if throbbing pain prevents sleep.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Deep infections in the finger spaces can develop rapidly; monitor closely for escalating throbbing pain.
Clinical Course: The sharp stinging will subside within 24 hours. The tissue adhesive will naturally flake off in 5 to 10 days as the underlying skin heals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if a red streak starts moving up your hand, or if the wound discharges foul-smelling yellow fluid.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Tissue glue forms a waterproof matrix that holds the clean edges together perfectly, avoiding the need for painful needle injections required for stitches.
Actionable Steps: Keep the finger dry for 24-48 hours. Buddy-tape the finger as shown to stop the skin from bending. Do not pick at the glue.

